{
  "dom_id": "step-q116321",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The irradiance $I,$ in $W/{m}^{2},$ of a lamp placed in the center of a spherical chamber varies inversely with the **square** of the radius $r$ of the spherical chamber, measured in meters. It is known that the irradiance is $0.6\\,W/{m}^{2}$ when the radius of the chamber is $3\\,m.$ What equation gives the relationship between $I$ and $r?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since the irradiance $I$ is inversely proportional to the square of the radius of the spherical chamber, $r$, we must have\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $I=0.6\\,W/{m}^{2}$ when $r=3\\,\\text{m}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.6 & =\\frac{k}{{3}^{2}} \\\\ 0.6 & =\\frac{k}{9} \\\\ 0.6\\cdot 9 & =k \\\\ 5.4 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{5.4}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$",
